.TH WM2TJ-BAR 1 "2026-10-22" "wm2tj-bar 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wm2tj-bar \- top bar for wm2tj and other EWMH window managers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm2tj-bar
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wm2tj-bar
is a top bar for the X Window System, written for wm2tj but usable with any
window manager that follows the EWMH specification: it talks to the window
manager only through EWMH properties.  It is one process, plain C and Xlib,
and reads CPU, memory, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and battery state straight from
.I /proc
and
.IR /sys .
.PP
From left to right: the application menu button, drawn as a wm2tj tab laid
on its side; the windows on the current desktop (click to raise, click again
to minimise); the virtual desktops (click or scroll); CPU and memory; the
system tray (XEmbed); Wi-Fi; the input method; Bluetooth; volume; battery;
and the clock.  Hovering over an icon shows the numbers.  Clicking most
icons opens a menu: Wi-Fi networks (with a password box of its own),
paired Bluetooth devices, sound outputs and mute, brightness and the power
profile.  The bar also handles the brightness and volume keys and switches
the power profile when the charger is plugged in or out.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from
.I ~/.config/wm2tj-bar/config
(see
.I config.sample
in the source distribution for every keyword) and re-read on
.BR SIGHUP .
The look of the menu button follows the tab settings in
.IR ~/.wm2rc ,
so the bar matches the window tabs.
.B wm2tj-bar-settings
edits the configuration in a window.
.PP
The bar runs these programs when they are installed, and hides the item
otherwise:
.B nmcli
(NetworkManager) for Wi-Fi,
.B wpctl
or
.B pactl
for volume,
.B bluetoothctl
for Bluetooth,
.B powerprofilesctl
for the power profile,
.B fcitx5-remote
or
.B ibus
for the input method, and
.BR systemctl / loginctl
for the session commands.
.PP
.B monitors
chooses the monitors that show the bar:
.B primary
(the default), 
.BR all ,
or monitor names as
.B xrandr \-\-listmonitors
prints them.  The bar itself, with the tray, sits on the primary monitor
(or the first one chosen); the others get a copy that acts just like it.
Only a bar at the top edge of the screen reserves a strut.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration:
.B pkill \-HUP \-x wm2tj-bar
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wm2tj-bar/config
Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/wm2tj-bar/favorites
Favourites of the application menu.  With
.B sync_wm2tj yes
the bar shares
.I ~/.config/wm2tj/favorites
with the wm2tj root menu instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wm2tj (1),
.BR nmcli (1),
.BR wpctl (1),
.BR bluetoothctl (1)
.PP
https://github.com/wm2tj/wm2tj
.SH AUTHOR
tetsu
